.SH NOTES
.SH Cygwin SO_PEERCRED
Cygwin emulates \f[CR]AF_UNIX\f[R] sockets over TCP loopback
(\f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R]).
To authenticate peers, it performs a credential handshake during
\f[CR]connect()\f[R]/\f[CR]accept()\f[R] that exchanges a secret and
\f[CR]ucred\f[R] structure.
.PP
CPython\(cqs \f[CR]socket.connect()\f[R] uses a non\-blocking connect +
poll + \f[CR]getsockopt(SO_ERROR)\f[R] pattern that races with this
handshake, causing \f[CR]ECONNABORTED\f[R] (errno 113) on
\f[CR]accept()\f[R].
.PP
Calling \f[CR]setsockopt(SO_PEERCRED, NULL, 0)\f[R] disables the
handshake entirely (\f[CR]af_local_set_no_getpeereid\f[R] in Cygwin\(cqs
\f[CR]net.cc\f[R]), allowing Python clients to connect reliably.
The tradeoff is that \f[CR]getpeereid()\f[R] and
\f[CR]getsockopt(SO_PEERCRED)\f[R] will no longer return peer
credentials.
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R]: The \f[CR]NULL, 0\f[R] form is required.
Passing non\-NULL \f[CR]optval\f[R] or non\-zero \f[CR]optlen\f[R]
returns \f[CR]EINVAL\f[R].
.SH Security
The socket file is created with \f[CR]0600\f[R] permissions, restricting
access to the owning user.
The parent directory should also be restricted (the server creates it
with \f[CR]0700\f[R]).
Combined with the auth token, this provides defense in depth against
local unauthorized access.
